{-
   ==========================================================================================
   `Environment` - the command line and the environment - Design-Test.md §11.2's F4.
   ==========================================================================================

   The half of F4 that is not about spawning: reading what this process was started with. A test
   binary needs it for `-filter`, `-shard`, `-isolate` and every other switch §6 gives the runner,
   and nothing in the language could reach it - "no way to read the command line" is the gap that row
   names.

   **The kernel hands these to the process on its stack and to nothing else.** So the values are read
   by the *entry stub* - hand-written bytes in the amd64 backend, eleven lines of assembly in the
   module the LLVM one hands to the linker - into three globals of `Native`, and everything here
   reads those. That sidesteps rather than fixes "the
   entry point cannot take arguments yet", which is the honest description of it: `main` still takes
   none, and what a program reads instead is a global filled before it started.

   **A vector is NUL-terminated and so is each of its strings**, which is the C convention: the count
   for the arguments comes from the kernel, and the environment's end is a null pointer. Each string
   is copied out on the way, because a `String` over the kernel's own bytes would be a run this
   program does not own and cannot resize.

   **`.native.yana`, so on JavaScript this module does not exist**, on the terms §11.2's F4 note
   gives: the JS target gets the runner and not the harness, and orchestration happens in a native
   parent that spawns `node`. A `process.argv` wrapper here would be a second answer to a question
   the design settles in the other direction, and half a `Process` module on one target is exactly
   the lie that note is written against.
-}

import Native

{-
   How many arguments this process was given, including the program's own name at position 0.

   Zero in a build whose stub wrote nothing, which is every build where nothing reads these globals
   at all: a global nothing reaches is not emitted, and the stub then has nowhere to store into. That
   is not a case to guard against - a program that never asks cannot observe the difference.
-}
pub fn argumentCount() -> Size = commandLineCount

{-
   One argument, by position, copied out of the kernel's own bytes.

   `Nothing` past the end rather than a trap: the count is the caller's to check, and this is the
   answer that composes with a loop that got it wrong.
-}
pub fn argument(index: Size) -> Maybe(String):
    if index >= commandLineCount then return Nothing
    return Just(stringFromCString(*(commandLineValues + index)))

-- Every argument, in order. One array and one string per argument, which is what a caller that is
-- about to parse them wants and is why this is not an iterator over the vector.
pub fn arguments() -> [String]:
    let count = commandLineCount
    let &all = [] :: [String]
    let &i = 0 :: Size

    while i < count:
        push(all, stringFromCString(*(commandLineValues + i)))
        i = i + 1

    return all

{-
   One environment variable, by name.

   A linear walk of the vector rather than a map built once, and the reason is what this is for: a
   program reads two or three of these at startup and never again, so a map would be an allocation
   and a hash of every variable to answer a question asked three times.

   The comparison runs against the kernel's bytes directly rather than against a `String` copy of
   each entry, which is the difference between one allocation for the answer and one per variable in
   the environment. It is also what makes the match exact: the name has to be followed by `=`, so
   `PATHEXT` does not answer for `PATH`.
-}
pub fn variable(name: String) -> Maybe(String):
    let &at = commandLineEnvironment
    if isNull(at) then return Nothing

    while (bitcast(*at) :: USize) != 0:
        match matchVariable(*at, name):
            Just(->value) -> return Just(value)
            Nothing -> {}

        at = at + 1

    return Nothing

-- Whether one `NAME=value` entry is this name's, and its value if it is.
fn matchVariable(entry: %U8, name: String) -> Maybe(String):
    let count = length(name)
    let &i = 0 :: Size

    while i < count:
        if *(entry + i) != (truncate(String.unit(name, i)) :: U8) then return Nothing
        i = i + 1

    if *(entry + count) != (61 :: U8) then return Nothing
    return Just(stringFromCString(entry + count + 1))
